ounding regions, an area well suited for grain, vineyards, and livestock. The land fulfilled God’s covenant promise to 
Abraham and gave each clan within Issachar a lasting place among the tribes of Israel. Jacob had portrayed Issachar as 
a strong donkey content to bear burdens in a good land (Genesis 49:14–15), and the tribe’s inheritance reflects both ag
ricultural abundance and the labor required to cultivate and defend it. The division by clans preserved family identity
, property rights, and covenant responsibility within Israel.
